[R0652] year=2026 | venue=2026 7th International Conference on Big Data & Artificial Intelligence & Software Engineering (ICBASE) | type=CONF
TITLE: On the Limited Effect of Role Prompting in LLM-based Code Vulnerability Detection
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Generative Pre-trained transformer; Codes; Signal detection; Labeling; Cognition; Cognitive systems; Security; Printing; Gain; Large language models; code vulnerability detection; role prompting
ABSTRACT: Role prompting is a widely adopted prompt engineering strategy that assigns specific personas to large language models (LLMs) to guide their reasoning processes. While prior studies have demonstrated its effectiveness on reasoning benchmarks, its impact on code vulnerability detection remains unclear. In this paper, we present a systematic empirical study on the effect of role prompting in LLM-based vulnerability detection under a constrained binary-output setting. We evaluate six representative LLMs-including DeepSeek-V3, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-5, Claude Sonnet 4.6, and Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-under seven role settings (four relevant roles, two irrelevant roles, and a no-role baseline) across three CWE categories from the Big-Vul dataset. The results show that simple role-label prompting does not consistently improve detection performance: for most models, the F1-score difference between the best relevant role and the no-role baseline remains within 0.05. Furthermore, irrelevant roles sometimes achieve performance comparable to relevant ones, and role prompting primarily affects the Precision-Recall trade-off rather than enhancing vulnerability understanding. Statistical significance testing using McNemar’s test with Holm-Bonferroni correction across all six models further confirms that none of the 42 Overall-scope comparisons reach significance at $\alpha=0.05$. These findings suggest that assigning brief role labels alone is insufficient to reliably improve LLM-based vulnerability detection in binary classification settings, and more structured prompting strategies deserve further investigation.

[R0653] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Applied Earth Observations and Remote Sensing | type=JOUR
TITLE: QL-Sec: Quantum AI-Enabled Security Framework for LLM-Driven Low-Altitude Remote Sensing Networks
KEYWORDS: Remote sensing; Autonomous aerial vehicles; Educational institutions; Modeling; Privacy; Security; Large language models; Telemetry; Noise; Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; LLMs; Quantum AI; Remote Sensing; UAVs
ABSTRACT: Low-altitude remote sensing network technology has advanced rapidly, especially with the assistance of large language models (LLMs). The use of LLMs, especially with unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) in remote sensing systems, poses significant security and privacy risks due to the need to transmit sensitive information and to enable large-scale distributed communication. This paper introduces the Quantum AI Security Framework (QL Sec) for LLM-powered low-altitude remote sensing networks. The proposed solution utilizes a hybrid Quantum AI security mechanism designed to enhance confidentiality, integrity, and authentication, and to resist adversarial intrusions. The framework also uses quantum-secured encryption and privacy-preserving feature perturbation to help reduce information leakage during edge-cloud communication. Results reveal that the proposed framework achieves 96.41% attack detection accuracy, a secret key rate (SKR) of 0.91 bits/use, and a reduced quantum bit error rate (QBER) of 0.029. SHAP explainability analysis validates the contribution of quantum-secure and quantum-safe components to trustworthy decision-making. The proposed Quantum AI-based system provides an efficient, scalable, and highly secure solution for future intelligent low-altitude remote sensing systems.

[R0654] year=2026 | venue=2026 Global Conference on Wireless and Optical Technologies (GCWOT) | type=CONF
TITLE: Dataset for DDoS Detection in 5G-Enabled Connected and Autonomous Vehicle Systems
KEYWORDS: 5G mobile communication; Denial-of-service attack; Modeling; Vehicles; Printing; Autonomous vehicles; Simulation; Distributed denial-of-service attack; Timing; Equations; Autonomous Vehicles; 5G; Cybersecurity; DDoS attacks; GPS; CARLA simulator; Gen-AI; ML; DL; Intelligent transportation system
ABSTRACT: The intelligent Autonomous Vehicles (AVs) rely on 5G connectivity to exchange positional data, perception outputs, and control commands in real time. This communication enables coordinated motion and safety-critical response. However, it also increases the environment's exposure to Distributed Denial-ofService (DDoS) attacks, disrupting message flow and impairing operational safety. To investigate this risk, a simulated driving-and-DDoS-attack scenario is developed in the CARLA simulator under a 5 G -enabled AV setting. This setup yields the UL-ECE-5G-AV-DDoS2025 dataset, which contains regular driving traffic and DDoS attack traffic. The dataset includes Global Positioning System (GPS) coordinates, speed, acceleration, throttle, steering, braking values, and network-layer metrics. Hence, this UL-ECE-5G-AV-DDoS2025 dataset supports reproducible benchmarking for Generative-AI (Gen-AI), machine learning, deep learning, and traffic-flow analysis in 5 G -connected transport environments.

[R0655] year=2026 | venue=2026 29th International Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work in Design (CSCWD) | type=CONF
TITLE: ShadowPen: Synergizing Human Strategy with Proactive Shadow Agent for XSS Testing
KEYWORDS: Security; Printing; Modeling; Artificial intelligence; Manuals; Tools; Large language models; Conferences; Shadow mapping; Fluid flow; Cross-Site Scripting (XSS); Human-AI Collaboration; Mixed-Initiative Interface; Cognitive Load; Security Testing; Large Language Models (LLMs)
ABSTRACT: Detecting Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in modern web applications challenges both automated scanners and human testers. Scanners often fail to handle complex business logic, while experts suffer high cognitive load from repetitive tasks such as endpoint enumeration and payload modification. To address these gaps, we propose ShadowPen, a collaborative framework combining human strategic planning with a proactive Shadow Agent. ShadowPen utilizes context-aware crawlers and Large Language Models (LLMs) to construct a structured decision workspace, which significantly reduces the initial burden on testers. To preserve expert cognitive flow, the agent operates non-intrusively by silently observing failed attempts and autonomously refining adaptive payload variants in the background, reporting only valid findings to augment rather than distract the user. Experiments with 12 participants across 10 diverse environments demonstrate that our approach significantly decreases the time to verify vulnerabilities compared to baseline tools. Furthermore, the system measurably reduces user cognitive load and frustration, confirming the value of human-AI synergy in security testing. Our code is available at https://github.com/Cbdlll/ShadowPen.

