ABSTRACT: Hackers are now beginning to use artificial intelligence (AI) to expand the capabilities and sophistication of their attacks, creating challenges for network owners. As state-sponsored adversaries and advanced persistent threats (APTs) utilize AI to their advantage, network owners must evolve concurrently to address the emergent threat landscape shaped by advanced AI systems. Thus, in this paper, we propose an end-to-end Machine Learning Framework to automate and strengthen cybersecurity operations. To demonstrate the feasibility of our approach, in this work-in-progress paper, we built and evaluated, a framework that consists of a machine learning (ML) model that automates the classifications of alerts generated by the Splunk Security Information and Event Management system (SIEM) to map adversary behaviors to MITRE ATT&CK stages. Our automated detection efforts are then fed into an LLM to generate an incident response report for cyber threat intelligence (CTI) and incident response teams. Our experimental results are intended to highlight the feasibility of combining adversary emulation with early real-time AI-assisted detection to strengthen organizational resilience (i.e. in Critical Infrastructure) against sophisticated campaigns.

[R0658] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Cybersecurity, Digital Forensics, and AI Applications (ICCSDFAI) | type=CONF
TITLE: Development and Cybersecurity Evaluation of an AI-Based Clinical Decision Support Portal for Blood Gas Analysis and Secure Patient Data Management
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Blood; Medical services; Gases; Artificial intelligence; Large language models; Manuals; Computer security; Security; Portals; clinical decision support system; blood gas analysis; cybersecurity; patient data protection; large language model; medical artificial intelligence; medical data security; privacy-preserving AI; data anonymization; electronic medical records
ABSTRACT: The growing adoption of artificial intelligence in healthcare improves diagnostic accuracy and clinical decision support but also creates significant challenges in protecting sensitive patient data. This paper presents an AI-based clinical decision support portal for blood gas analysis and secure patient data management, designed for the diagnosis of diabetic and hematologic syndromes using arterial blood gas analysis. The portal is implemented as a modular full-stack platform based on ReactJS, Express.js, Sequelize ORM, and PostgreSQL, integrating both local and remote large language models for clinical text interpretation and diagnostic reasoning. The study focuses on secure system architecture and privacy-preserving design for AI-enabled healthcare applications. A comprehensive cybersecurity analysis addresses threats such as unauthorized access, API abuse, credential compromise, prompt injection, and risks of sensitive data leakage during external LLM interaction. To mitigate these risks, a multi-layered security framework is implemented, including JWT-based authentication, role-based access control, encrypted credential storage, rate limiting, secure deployment practices, and network isolation. A local anonymization module removes personally identifiable information before any interaction with external LLM services, significantly reducing privacy risks while preserving the effectiveness of AI-assisted diagnostics. The proposed architecture demonstrates a secure and scalable approach to integrating AI into clinical decision support systems and highlights the importance of combining explainable AI with strong cybersecurity mechanisms in modern digital healthcare.

[R0659] year=2024 | venue=2024 Artificial Intelligence Revolutions (AIR) | type=CONF
TITLE: Generative AI-Based Tool for Brute Forcing IoT Devices' Default Credentials
