ABSTRACT: This study beneficially uses the power of generative AI to search for vendor-specific default credentials and uses them to brute force IoT devices logins. IoT devices have a diverse set of open ports used for accessing and configuration. With the increased usage of IoT devices, keeping all devices' ports wellsecured is overwhelming and costly, especially for SMEs. Using a variety of methods to approach the problem, this research studied IoT attacks, characteristics, IoT penetration tools, and small to medium size enterprises (SMEs) requirements to produce an automated solution. Findings indicated that a lot of IoT devices are still configured with default credentials making the networks they are connected to vulnerable attacks. The solution presented, is a script that integrates OpenAI GPT to search for default credentials, Nmap to scan for open ports, and Hydra to attack the device. The tool is implemented to assess some specific widely used ports. To detect vulnerable IoT devices and report them to the user, the tool analyses login pages available on ports 80 and 443 to search for the brand and model of the IoT device. The output is used for the default credentials GPT search. Despite its ability to shortlist the dictionary for a brute force list, it should be tested on an experiential environment that includes different IoT simulators with several open ports on changed credentials and default ones. Then verified its functionality on a real IoT network. Further research could explore implementing machine learning to thoroughly analyse IoT device firmware.

[R0660] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 31st International Conference on Emerging Technologies and Factory Automation (ETFA) | type=CONF
TITLE: VISOR: Automated Vulnerability Assessment of Software Repositories
KEYWORDS: Tools; Modeling; Printing; Software; Large language models; Codes; Cognition; Cognitive systems; Security; Computer security; Software Supply Chain Security; Vulnerability Assessment; Agentic AI; Multi-agent LLMs
ABSTRACT: Modern software systems increasingly depend on open-source and commercial third-party components, which can expose them to cybersecurity risks present within these external dependencies. The vulnerability assessment of such components remains predominantly manual, creating bottlenecks that slow remediation efforts. This work introduces VISOR, an automated, LLM-driven framework that collects vulnerability information from various online sources and orchestrates the selection and invocation of multiple custom-designed vulnerability-assessment tools to generate structured and actionable reports for human experts. The framework supports two workflows: a linear agentic workflow that generates and then refines a well-parameterized tool plan, and a non-linear agentic workflow that adaptively revises the analysis through iterative refinement, dynamic tool selection, and evidence-guided adjustments. At its core, VISOR collects and correlates evidence to determine whether a reported vulnerability is applicable to a target software repository and whether the vulnerable code is reachable within the codebase. By design, the framework does not automatically apply mitigations to fix vulnerabilities; instead, it assists cybersecurity experts by providing evidence-based assessments and proposed mitigation actions in a structured report, enabling informed remediation decisions. Experiments show that the framework achieves 76% agreement with cybersecurity expert assessments while substantially reducing manual effort during initial vulnerability triage.

[R0661] year=2026 | venue=NAECON 2026 - IEEE National Aerospace and Electronics Conference | type=CONF
TITLE: Analyzing Structural and Semantic Barriers to Automated Adversary Emulation in Active Directory
KEYWORDS: Tools; Printing; Large language models; Modeling; Security; Friction; Synchronization; Architecture; Computer architecture; Protocols; Active Directory; Autonomous Agents; Cyber Operations; Large Language Models
ABSTRACT: Active Directory (AD) underpins enterprise identity management and is among the most heavily targeted assets in modern enterprise networks. When red teams emulate attackers targeting it, they typically chain BloodHound for relationship mapping, Impacket for protocol manipulation, and Responder for credential interception. Despite rapid advances in Large Language Models (LLMs), reliably automating this BIR workflow is still difficult, and the difficulty is not primarily one of reasoning. We analyze three sources of integration friction that block automation. The first is data dependency and contextual fragility, the second is temporal and procedural synchronization, and the third is semantic and operational inconsistency. We propose three architectural responses. They are tool wrappers, sidecar validators, and a shared Blackboard. Throughout, we carefully separate what we validate from what we propose. We empirically evaluate the sidecar pattern across four failure modes $(N=80)$. The wrapper and Blackboard layers are presented as architecture whose end-to-end validation remains future work. For the sidecar, deterministic pre-validation lets code, rather than the LLM, route around connectivity failures, with equivalent latency and only a modest token overhead in the structured output. The same experiment serves as a component-level ablation. It contrasts workflow behavior with and without the sidecar, converting ambiguous tool errors into structured, actionable directives.

[R0662] year=2025 | venue=SoutheastCon 2025 | type=CONF
TITLE: Unified AI and ML Framework in DevSecOps Practices, Solving Real-World Problems
KEYWORDS: Industries; Root cause analysis; Codes; Digital transformation; Standards organizations; Transforms; Security; Monitoring; Anomaly detection; Testing; Artificial Intelligence; Machine Learning; IAC; GitOps; CI/CD; SDLC; MLOps; Platform Ops; Healthcare; DevOps
ABSTRACT: Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) are evolving daily and advancing human progression in every field of endeavor like arts, science, and business. Information Technology (IT) is the field where this transformation is most accelerated due to its role in enabling digital transformation across other industries. DevSecOps is the standard set of practices that emerged as the ideal solution for managing the software development life cycle in this digital transformation journey. DevSecOps practices are also evolving along with the IT industry, and there are challenges and opportunities to improve continuously. This research paper introduces a conceptual framework for implementing a unified AI and ML solution to optimize the DevSecOps practice of any organization and constantly improve. This framework aims to solve some of the most challenging problems in DevSecOps practices by accelerating software development, reducing defects, increasing security scanning accuracy, enhancing automation, improving performance testing, and streamlining operations. Our solution accomplishes this using consolidated DevSecOps data-driven predictive analytics output from the Unified AI and ML model. By introducing this conceptual framework, the paper encourages more research and development in the core area of DevSecOps practices, which function as the cornerstone of digital transformation across all industries.

[R0663] year=2026 | venue=2026 Seventeenth International Conference on Ubiquitous and Future Networks (ICUFN) | type=CONF
TITLE: HG-Probe: A Safety-Bounded Evidence Layer for LLM-Guided Web/API Security Testing
KEYWORDS: Probes; Mercury (metals); Safety; Large language models; Application programming interfaces; Testing; Concrete; Context; Costing; Costs; web security; API security; DAST; gray-box testing; adaptive probing; evidence confirmation
ABSTRACT: Large language model (LLM)-assisted security testing agents for networked web and application programming interface (API) services are useful for planning, prioritization, and vulnerability reasoning, but allowing such agents to directly generate and execute arbitrary web/API probes raises safety, reproducibility, and evidence-validity risks. This paper presents HG-Probe (Hypothesis-Guided Probe), a safety-bounded execution and evidence-validation layer for LLM-guided web/API dynamic application security testing (DAST). HG-Probe still lets an external agent prioritize endpoint-parameter pairs or suggest high-level hypotheses, but the actual HTTP actions are chosen internally from an allowlisted probe set, filtered by the safety policy, and reported only when the corresponding evidence rule is satisfied. In controlled and scoped benchmarks, HG-Probe reduces request cost relative to static schedules while preserving precision against scoped ground-truth manifests, and agent-guided candidate ordering reduces time-to-first safe-confirmed evidence without allowing the agent to bypass the safety boundary.

[R0664] year=2026 | venue=2026 Twelfth International Conference on eDemocracy & eGovernment (ICEDEG) | type=CONF
TITLE: LLM-Powered Brute Force Attacks: An Iterative SSDLC Approach for Authentication Process Evasion
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Signal detection; Large language models; Passwords; Timing; Authentication; Testing; Security; Dictionaries; Printing; framework; cybersecurity maturity; digital ecosystem; privacy
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed the ability of attackers to compromise authentication processes through the autonomous generation of adaptive attacks. Objectives: This research aims to develop computational cognitive profiles that simulate human behavior to bypass automated detection, and to empirically analyze password patterns based on the Ecuadorian context. Method: We employed an iterative Secure Software Development Life Cycle (SSDLC) methodology across four iterations to develop and validate these offensive and defensive contributions. Results: We developed three differentiated cognitive profiles (reflective novice, continuous expert, and erratic automated) validated against temporal distributions of human interaction, achieving a 91% evasion rate of detection systems. The analysis of 207 passwords reveals exploitable cultural patterns: 66% incorporate numerical suffixes, 20% include local contextual references, and the average length is 10.5 characters. This work establishes the foundations for resilient authentication systems that consider cognitive and cultural factors.

